<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint Petersburg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as St. Petersburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint Petersburg is a city in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neva-river">Neva River</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> in 1703 on territory conquered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. It was built as a purpose-built European capital and served as the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> for most of its history. Accounts of the Russian Empire treat its foundation as a symbol of Peter's westernising reforms and Russia's turn towards the Baltic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg.jpg" alt="Saint Petersburg" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Florstein (Telegram:WikiPhoto.Space) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peter_%26_Paul_fortress_in_SPB_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bosporan workshops produced much of the Greco-Scythian gold work now in the Hermitage Museum in <b>Saint Petersburg</b>, and the kurgans of Kerch and the Taman peninsula became central to the archaeology of the region from the nineteenth century onwards.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bosporan-kingdom">Bosporan Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>A congress at Hlukhiv proposing a hereditary hetmanship and a return to Khmelnytsky's terms alarmed <b>Saint Petersburg</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Moscow lost its status as capital to <b>St Petersburg</b> in the early eighteenth century and regained it in 1918.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Russian Orthodox Church to the state by replacing the Patriarchate with the Holy Synod in 1721, founding <b>Saint Petersburg</b> as a purpose-built European capital on conquered Swedish territory from 1703, and introducing the Table of Ranks in 1722, which tied noble status to state service rather than…</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Second World War it bore much of the fighting after Germany invaded on 22 June 1941: <b>Leningrad</b> endured a siege of almost 900 days, the Germans were stopped before Moscow in December 1941, and the battles of Stalingrad in 1942–1943 and Kursk in 1943 turned the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Peter founded <b>Saint Petersburg</b> on the Neva in 1703, building a fleet and a new capital to which the court moved during the 1710s, and created a conscript army maintained by heavy taxation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
